// lowerthird-demo — a broadcast-style lower third for compositing over video: the lower
// third of the frame filled with translucent black, the rest of the frame fully
// transparent, and normally-flowing white text set inside the band. It combines three
// pieces — \pagecolor{transparent} (an unpainted page, so everything outside the band
// shows the video through it), \colorbox[alpha] (the translucent fill), and a
// fixed-height \parbox (the band keeps its size no matter how much text it holds).
//
// Render as a transparent PNG at exactly 1280x720:
//   texish -t png -r sd scripts/lowerthird-demo.script
// At -r sd (96 dpi) points scale x4/3 to pixels, so the 960x540pt frame below is a
// 1280x720 image; for 1080p use 1440x810pt instead.

\use{document}

\geometry paperwidth:960pt paperheight:540pt margin:0
\pagecolor{transparent}

// Push the band to the foot of the frame.
\vfill

// The band spans the full width and is a fixed 180pt tall — one third of 540. \fboxsep
// gives it its inner padding; leftskip/rightskip indent the text further inside. The
// parbox width plus twice \fboxsep equals the frame width, so the fill runs edge to edge.
\set fboxsep {30}
\colorbox[0.55]{black}{\parbox[c][120pt][c]{900pt}{
\color{white}
\set leftskip {30}
\set rightskip {30}
{\bfseries\large A Lower Third}\par
\medskip
Text flows here as it would in any paragraph — justified, hyphenated, and wrapping to
the width of the band. A second paragraph follows the first with normal spacing, the
whole block held at a fixed height regardless of how much or how little it holds.
}}
